  "question_text": "What is the cognitive revolution in psychology?",
  "generated_answer": "The cognitive revolution was a mid‑20th‑century shift in psychology that moved the focus from observable behavior to the mental processes that underlie it. Emerging in the 1950s, it was driven by new insights from linguistics, neuroscience, and computer science, and it revived scientific interest in the mind. This interdisciplinary movement, influenced by figures such as Noam Chomsky and Ulric Neisser, led to the development of cognitive psychology and the broader field of cognitive science. It replaced the dominant behaviorist emphasis on external behavior with an emphasis on internal cognition, perception, and information processing.",
